- **11 total font weights are requested** (Barlow Condensed: 6 weights, Barlow: 5 weights). Each weight is a separate WOFF2 file (~15-30KB). On a cold cache, this can be 200-300KB of font downloads. Many of these weights may not be used (e.g., Barlow 300, Barlow Condensed 900 in body text).
- **`display=swap` IS present**, which ensures FOUT (flash of fallback text) rather than FOIT (invisible text). This is good.
- **No `<link rel="preconnect">` for Google Fonts origins.** Without `preconnect` hints, the browser does not learn about `fonts.googleapis.com` or `fonts.gstatic.com` until it parses the `@import`.

**Recommendation:** Replace `@import` with `<link>` tags in `<head>` and add preconnect hints. For example, add to `<head>` of each page:
```html
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com">
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin>
<link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Barlow+Condensed:wght@400;600;700;900&family=Barlow:wght@400;500;600;700&display=swap" rel="stylesheet">
```
Also consider trimming unused font weights. The CSS only uses Barlow Condensed 700, 800, 900 for headings and Barlow 400 for body text, so some of the requested weights (e.g., Barlow 300, Barlow Condensed 400/500/600) may be unused.

---

## 2. CSS Performance Issues

### `transition: all` on five different rule sets

| File | Line | Selector |
|------|------|----------|
| `styles.css` | 220 | `.btn` |
| `styles.css` | 561 | `.card` |
| `styles.css` | 630 | `.way-card` |
| `styles.css` | 1015 | `.instructor-card` |
| `styles.css` | 1251 | `.feature-card` |

Using `transition: all` forces the browser to watch every animatable property for changes. In practice, these cards only change `border-color`, `box-shadow`, and `transform` on hover. Specifying only those properties allows the browser to skip unnecessary work:

```css
/* Instead of: */
transition: all var(--duration-base) var(--ease-out);

/* Use: */
transition: transform var(--duration-base) var(--ease-out),
            border-color var(--duration-base) var(--ease-out),
            box-shadow var(--duration-base) var(--ease-out);
```

### FAQ accordion animates `max-height` (styles.css, lines 1150-1157)

```css
.faq-answer {
  max-height: 0;
  transition: max-height 0.4s var(--ease-out), padding 0.4s var(--ease-out);
}
.faq-item.open .faq-answer {
  max-height: 300px;
}
```

Animating `max-height` triggers **forced layout** (reflow) on every frame of the animation. The browser must recalculate element dimensions continuously. A fixed `max-height` value like 300px also risks clipping long content. A performant alternative would be to use JavaScript to measure the actual height and animate with `transform` / `clip-path`, or use the `grid-template-rows: 0fr / 1fr` technique that modern browsers can composite.

### `backdrop-filter: blur(20px)` on sticky nav (styles.css, line 301)

```css
.nav {
  position: sticky;
  top: 0;
  ...
  backdrop-filter: blur(20px);
}
```

`backdrop-filter` is **paint-heavy** and the sticky nav means it re-paints on every scroll. Combined with `background: rgba(10, 10, 10, 0.92)`, the blur is largely masked by the near-opaque background, making the blur effect mostly imperceptible. Consider reducing the blur radius or removing it.

### `will-change: transform` on carousel track (styles.css, line 768)

This is used correctly on `.carousel-track` -- good practice for composited animations.

---

## 3. JavaScript Performance

### No throttled scroll/resize listeners -- GOOD

There are **no scroll or resize event listeners** anywhere in the codebase. This is clean.

### Instructor filter has a redundant second loop (instructors.html, lines 267-288)

```javascript
// First loop -- hides non-matching, shows matching, counts visible
cards.forEach(card => {
  if (filter === 'all' || card.getAttribute('data-location') === filter) {
    card.classList.remove('fade-out');
    card.style.display = '';
    visible++;
  } else {
    card.classList.add('fade-out');
    setTimeout(() => { if (card.classList.contains('fade-out')) { card.style.display = 'none'; } }, 300);
  }
});

// Second loop -- immediately UNDOES the fade-out for matching cards
cards.forEach(card => {
  if (filter === 'all' || card.getAttribute('data-location') === filter) {
    card.classList.remove('fade-out');
    card.style.display = '';
  }
});
```

The second loop (lines 283-288) is **completely redundant**. The first loop already sets `display: ''` and removes `fade-out` from matching cards. The second loop just does the same work again, doubling the iteration cost. Remove lines 283-288.

### Mobile nav `querySelectorAll` re-queried on every link click (all pages)

```javascript
navLinks.querySelectorAll('a').forEach(link => {
  link.addEventListener('click', () => { ... });
});
```

`querySelectorAll` returns a live NodeList, but it still traverses the DOM each time it is called. This is only called once on page load, so the impact is minimal. However, the same loop runs on every page -- and on desktop where the mobile menu is never visible, these 4 click handlers still fire (toggling classes on hidden elements). The cost is negligible but unnecessary.

### Carousel `mousemove` handler fires on every mouse move (index.html, lines 338-341)

```javascript
window.addEventListener('mousemove', e => {
  if (!dragging) return;
  diffX = e.clientX - startX;
});
```

This handler is registered permanently on `window` and fires on **every mouse movement across the entire page**, even when not dragging. The `if (!dragging) return` check is inside the handler, meaning the function is entered hundreds of times per second. A better pattern is to only attach `mousemove`/`mouseup` during drag (on `mousedown`) and remove them on `mouseup`.

---

## 4. Image / Asset Loading

**There are no `<img>` tags in any of the five pages.** All visual elements are CSS gradients, background colors, or text placeholders (e.g., instructor initials "DR", "MC" rendered as oversized text in a colored box). This means:

- **No `loading="lazy"` issues** (no images to lazy-load).
- **No `width`/`height` or CLS issues** from images.
- **No image network requests**, which is one less performance concern.

That said, replacing the text–based placeholders with actual photos (instructor headshots, studio photos, etc.) would improve visual appeal but would require careful attention to lazy-loading and aspect-ratio containment.

---

## 5. Animation Performance

### Good patterns being used:
- **Carousel** (index.html): Uses `transform: translateX()` via inline style -- this is a **composited property** that does not trigger layout or paint. `will-change: transform` is set in the CSS.
- **Toggle content fade-in** (the-workout.html): `@keyframes fadeSlideIn` animates only `opacity` and `transform` -- both composited.
- **Card hover effects**: `transform: translateY(-2px)` / `translateY(-4px)` -- composited.
- **Instructor filter fade-out**: `opacity` + `transform: scale(0.95)` -- composited.
- **Button hover**: `transform: translateY(-1px)` -- composited.

### Problematic patterns:
- **FAQ `max-height` animation** (ride-faq.html, shown via styles.css lines 1150-1157): Triggers layout on every frame. (See section 2 above.)
- **`backdrop-filter: blur(20px)` on sticky nav**: Triggers painting on scroll. (See section 2 above.)

---

## 6. Redundant Computations: Duplicated JavaScript

**The same ~68 lines of JavaScript are duplicated across all 5 pages:**

| Block | Lines per page | Present in |
|-------|---------------|-----------|
| Mobile nav toggle + link close handlers | ~20 | All 5 files |
| Newsletter validation (IIFE with blur/input/submit handlers) | ~48 | All 5 files |

**Total:** ~340 lines of duplicated JavaScript across the site.

**Is this a performance concern?** In terms of execution cost, not really -- each page parses and executes its own ~68 lines once. The browser caches the parsed script only within each page load.

**Is this a maintenance concern?** Yes. If the email validation regex changes, or if the newsletter endpoint URL is added, or if the mobile nav behavior is modified, **all 5 files must be updated identically**. This is a high-risk duplication.

**Recommendation:** Extract the shared code into a single external file (e.g., `/app/output/js/app.js`) loaded via `<script src="js/app.js">` at the bottom of each page. The newsletter and mobile nav code can be initialized based on the presence of DOM elements:
```javascript
// js/app.js
(function() {
  // Mobile nav
  const navMenuBtn = document.getElementById('navMenuBtn');
  const navLinks = document.getElementById('navLinks');
  if (navMenuBtn && navLinks) {
    navMenuBtn.addEventListener('click', () => { ... });
    navLinks.querySelectorAll('a').forEach(link => {
      link.addEventListener('click', () => { ... });
    });
  }

  // Newsletter
  const form = document.getElementById('newsletterForm');
  if (form) {
    // newsletter code...
  }
})();
```

---

## 7. Memory / Event Listener Cleanup

### Carousel mouse drag listeners on `window` are never removed (index.html, lines 338-349)

```javascript
window.addEventListener('mousemove', e => { ... });
window.addEventListener('mouseup', () => { ... });
```

These are registered on page load and **never removed**. If the page were a single-page app where the carousel section could be dynamically removed and re-added, the old listeners would accumulate. For a static page, they live for the page's lifetime and are freed on navigation. Not a leak in this context, but a pattern that would cause leaks in a dynamic app.

### No other listener accumulation issues:
- All other event listeners (`click`, `blur`, `input`, `submit`, `touchstart`, `touchmove`, `touchend`, `keydown`) are attached to static DOM elements that persist for the page's lifetime.
- No `setInterval`, no observable/subscription patterns, no growing arrays or DOM elements.

### Global variable leakage (all pages)

```javascript
const navMenuBtn = document.getElementById('navMenuBtn');
const navLinks = document.getElementById('navLinks');
```

These are declared at the top level (not inside an IIFE), making them properties of `window`. For a simple marketing site this is not a practical concern, but it is a minor scope leak.

---

## 8. Network Efficiency

### Each page loads `styles.css` fresh

On the first page visit, the browser downloads and caches `styles.css`. On subsequent page navigations, the cached copy is used (assuming appropriate `Cache-Control` headers from the server). This is standard multi-page behavior and is not a problem -- the CSS is shared and cached.

### Payload reduction opportunities:

1. **Move `@import` to `<link>`** (see section 1) -- avoids the extra round trip caused by `@import`'s render-blocking waterfall.

2. **Trim unused font weights** (see section 1) -- potentially saves hundreds of KB.

3. **Minify `styles.css`** -- the file is 1392 lines of well-formatted CSS. A minifier (e.g., cssnano, clean-css) would reduce it substantially (likely 50-60% size reduction).

4. **Extract duplicated inline `<script>` blocks** into a shared external file (see section 6) -- while the individual script blocks are small (~2KB each compressed), extracting them enables better caching.

5. **Inline critical CSS** -- for the above-the-fold content, inline a small subset of styles and defer the full `styles.css`. This would improve First Contentful Paint. The page-specific styles (hero backgrounds, etc.) are already in `<style>` blocks, which is good.

### Duplicated HTML structure

The announcement bar, navigation, newsletter section, and footer are identical HTML across all 5 pages (~120 lines each). This is standard for multi-page sites without a templating engine. Not a network concern (it compresses well with gzip), but it is a maintenance burden if links or copy need updating.

---

## Summary Table

| # | Severity | Issue | Location | Lines |
|---|----------|-------|----------|-------|
| 1 | **High** | `@import` for Google Fonts is render-blocking; missing `preconnect` | `styles.css` | 7 |
| 2 | **High** | 11 font weights requested, many likely unused | `styles.css` | 7 |
| 3 | **High** | `transition: all` on 5 component rule sets -- should specify only changed properties | `styles.css` | 220, 561, 630, 1015, 1251 |
| 4 | **High** | FAQ `max-height` animation causes layout thrashing on every open/close | `styles.css` | 1150-1157 |
| 5 | **High** | ~340 lines of JavaScript (mobile nav + newsletter) duplicated verbatim across 5 pages | All 5 HTML files | See section 6 |
| 6 | **High** | Instructor filter has a redundant second loop that re-does the same work | `instructors.html` | 283-288 |
| 7 | **Medium** | `backdrop-filter: blur(20px)` on sticky nav causes repaints on every scroll | `styles.css` | 301 |
| 8 | **Medium** | Carousel `mousemove` handler on `window` fires on every mouse event page-wide, even when not dragging | `index.html` | 338-341 |
| 9 | **Medium** | Carousel mouse drag listeners on `window` are never cleaned up (pattern issue) | `index.html` | 338-349 |
| 10 | **Medium** | Mobile nav creates global `navMenuBtn`/`navLinks` on `window` (top-level `const`) | All 5 HTML files | Varies |
| 11 | **Low** | Minification of `styles.css` would reduce ~50%+ of its uncompressed size | `styles.css` | Entire file |
| 12 | **Low** | No real images used anywhere -- all visuals are CSS/text placeholders | All files | -- |

